\section{Introduction}
Bar\'e (ISO 639-3 \texttt{bae}) is an Arawakan language of the upper Rio Negro region of Venezuela and Brazil. Sources disagree on its status: Wikipedia reports the last fluent speaker in Brazil died in 1993, while other figures list a few hundred speakers. Aikhenvald (1999) reports only a few old speakers remaining.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found in the searches.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Bar\'e language, Wikipedia. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bar%C3%A9_language}
\item Christiane Cunha de Oliveira, \emph{Uma descri\c{c}\~ao do Bare (Arawak): aspectos fonol\'ogicos e gramaticais} (1993 master's thesis, UFSC). \url{https://repositorio.ufsc.br/handle/123456789/157788}
\item WALS language page for Bar\'e. \url{https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_bae}
\end{itemize}
Search results also mention A.\ Aikhenvald's grammar \emph{Bare} (Lincom Europa, Languages of the World/Materials 100, 1995 or 1996); no URL for it was seen, so it is not listed with a link.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Only linguistic descriptions were found, with no school materials. Any curriculum would depend on the existing grammatical descriptions and on work with the few remaining speakers.

\end{document}
